-- title:
--   Factorisation of bialgebra maps killed by a Hopf quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $O$ be a commutative ring and let $B$, $C$, $K$ be commutative rings equipped with Hopf $O$-algebra structures, each finite and free as an $O$-module. Let $\psi : B \to C$ be an $O$-bialgebra homomorphism and $q : C \to K$ a surjective $O$-bialgebra homomorphism whose kernel, as a ring homomorphism, is the ideal of $C$ generated by the image under $\psi$ of the kernel of the counit $\varepsilon_B : B \to O$, that is $\ker q = \psi(B^{+})C$. Assume the rank identity $\operatorname{rank}_O B \cdot \operatorname{rank}_O K = \operatorname{rank}_O C$ (as $O$-finranks). Let $k$ be a field with an $O$-algebra structure, and let $A$ be a commutative ring with a $k$-bialgebra structure. Assume that the base-changed map $\mathrm{id}_k \otimes \psi : k \otimes_O B \to k \otimes_O C$ is injective. Let $f : A \to k \otimes_O C$ be a $k$-bialgebra homomorphism such that $(\mathrm{id}_k \otimes q) \circ f$ equals, as a $k$-algebra homomorphism, the composite of the counit $\varepsilon_A : A \to k$ with the structure map $k \to k \otimes_O K$. Then there is a $k$-bialgebra homomorphism $g : A \to k \otimes_O B$ with $(\mathrm{id}_k \otimes \psi) \circ g = f$.
--
--   This is the Hopf-algebra form of the statement that a map of group schemes landing in the kernel of a quotient factors through the corresponding subgroup scheme, here in a fibrewise shape: the saturation of $\psi$ is replaced by injectivity of its base change to the field $k$, together with the multiplicativity of ranks. It is applied in the analysis of the Raynaud quotient of the level torsion of a modular curve, where a point-counting witness produces exactly such a bialgebra map annihilated by the quotient.
-- source:
